    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Get into position safely: sit with the dumbbells on the thighs, roll back with them held at the chest, and at the end of the set roll them back onto the thighs rather than dropping them beside the head (clinical opinion).",
        "Let the upper arms touch the floor softly; do not bang the elbows down or bounce off the floor.",
        "Keep the wrists straight over the forearms.",
        "Stop if sharp pain, numbness or tingling occurs.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Shoulder discomfort: neutral grip and the elbow closer to the side.",
        "Hold the free hand on the belly to feel the trunk staying square."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Two-arm dumbbell floor press.",
        "Lighter dumbbell."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Single-arm dumbbell bench press.",
        "Pause 2 s on the floor.",
        "Heavier dumbbell."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "pec_r": "Main pusher (horizontal adduction and flexion of the upper arm). The floor stops the elbows at about body level, so the stretched bottom of a bench press is left out (clinical opinion); with dumbbells, pectoralis activity matched barbell and Smith machine presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "pec_l": "Main pusher (horizontal adduction and flexion of the upper arm). The floor stops the elbows at about body level, so the stretched bottom of a bench press is left out (clinical opinion); with dumbbells, pectoralis activity matched barbell and Smith machine presses (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "triceps_r": "Straightens the elbows; with the range shortened to the upper part, the lockout share is relatively larger (clinical opinion).",
      "triceps_l": "Straightens the elbows; with the range shortened to the upper part, the lockout share is relatively larger (clinical opinion).",
      "ant_deltoid_r": "Flexes the shoulder with the chest.",
      "ant_deltoid_l": "Flexes the shoulder with the chest.",
      "serratus_r": "Holds the shoulder blades against the rib cage while each arm moves on its own.",
      "serratus_l": "Holds the shoulder blades against the rib cage while each arm moves on its own.",
      "biceps_r": "Stabilises the elbow; biceps activity rose with the stability demand and was highest with dumbbells (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "biceps_l": "Stabilises the elbow; biceps activity rose with the stability demand and was highest with dumbbells (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "forearm_flexors_r": "Grip the dumbbell handles.",
      "forearm_flexors_l": "Grip the dumbbell handles.",
      "infraspinatus_r": "Rotator cuff: centres the head of the upper arm while the dumbbells balance freely (clinical opinion).",
      "infraspinatus_l": "Rotator cuff: centres the head of the upper arm while the dumbbells balance freely (clinical opinion).",
      "rhomboids_midtrap_r": "Keep the shoulder blades drawn back and down on the floor.",
      "rhomboids_midtrap_l": "Keep the shoulder blades drawn back and down on the floor.",
      "core": "Bracing keeps the trunk square on the floor against the one-sided load (Saeterbakken 2012).",
      "obliques_r": "Resist the pull of the single dumbbell rolling the trunk toward the working side; unilateral presses raised trunk muscle activity versus bilateral ones (Saeterbakken 2012).",
      "obliques_l": "Resist the pull of the single dumbbell rolling the trunk toward the working side; unilateral presses raised trunk muscle activity versus bilateral ones (Saeterbakken 2012)."
    }
  },
